01407623291015625, 0.00736236572265625, -0.01441192626953125, -0.007472991943359375, 0.023773193359375, 0.04608154296875, 0.045318603515625, 0.030517578125, 0.0083770751953125, 0.028900146484375, 0.00533294677734375, -0.042449951171875, 0.004055023193359375, 0.004749298095703125, 0.018951416015625, -0.02203369140625, -0.00841522216796875, 0.011383056640625, -0.00983428955078125, -0.0380859375, 0.00572967529296875, -0.0160064697265625, 0.008514404296875, -0.01345062255859375, -0.02978515625, -0.02716064453125, 0.00873565673828125, -0.00600433349609375, -0.029144287109375, 0.019989013671875, -0.017547607421875, 0.035247802734375, 0.01558685302734375, -0.046600341796875, -0.0246734619140625, -0.0011997222900390625, 0.003437042236328125, -0.0222320556640625, 0.01345062255859375, 0.01122283935546875, -0.007740020751953125, -0.0005254745483398438, -0.0286712646484375, -0.0008764266967773438, 0.038787841796875, -0.005939483642578125, 0.01502227783203125, 0.01280975341796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits [1,2]. However, a central challenge remains: the transformation of the ordinary differential equations that govern a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process fundamental to system analysis and control design. While previous works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions [3] and Clifford algebras [4], these approaches either do not fully address the multigrade structure of GA or are limited to specific algebraic settings. For example, quaternionic Laplace transforms [3] typically treat only scalar and vector parts and are restricted to four-dimensional algebras, while Clifford algebra approaches [4] often assume commutativity or focus on monogenic functions, neglecting the full non-commutative, multigrade structure of general GA. In practical terms, this means that coupled scalar, vector, and higher-grade components\u2014common in physical systems\u2014cannot be handled in a unified way, limiting the expressiveness and applicability of these transforms. Thus, a comprehensive and consistent definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of Geometric Algebra remains lacking. Our work addresses this gap by providing a formal definition and analysis of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in GA, with explicit attention to the algebra\u2019s non-commutative structure.\n\nThe main contributions of this work are as follows: (1) we introduce a precise definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras of signature (p, q) with p + q \u2264 5; (2) we establish key properties of this transform, including linearity, the shifting theorem, and the treatment of non-commutative products; (3) we clarify the relationship and improvements over previous approaches for quaternions and Clifford algebras, highlighting the ability to handle all grades and non-commutative products; and (4) we demonstrate the practical benefits of our approach through a concrete example involving RLC circuit analysis, showing how geometric relationships are preserved.\n\nOur definition of the Laplace transform in GA is as follows: for a multivector-valued function \\( f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\), where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) is a geometric algebra of signature (p, q), the Laplace transform \\( \\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) \\) is defined by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex parameter. The exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) is interpreted as a scalar function of \\( t \\), and thus acts as a scalar multiplier on each grade component of \\( f(t) \\): if \\( f(t) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\langle f(t) \\rangle_k \\), then\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\langle f(t) \\rangle_k \\, dt.\n\\]\nSince \\( e^{-st} \\) is scalar-valued for real or complex \\( s \\), it commutes with all multivector components, ensuring the integral is well-defined. For complex \\( s \\), the scalar exponential remains commutative with all elements of \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) [see, e.g., 1, Ch. 2]. If \\( s \\) were allowed to be a general multivector, the non-commutativity of the geometric product would introduce ambiguities in the definition of the exponential and the integral. Therefore, our definition restricts \\( s \\) to scalar or complex values to avoid such issues.\n\nWe focus on geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This restriction is motivated primarily by computational tractability: as p + q increases, the dimension of the algebra grows exponentially (e.g., for p + q = 5, the algebra has 32 dimensions), making both analytical and numerical computations increasingly impractical. While there are no fundamental theoretical obstacles to extending the definition to higher dimensions, the complexity of manipulating and storing multivector-valued functions becomes prohibitive for most applications. Moreover, most physical and engineering applications, such as the spacetime algebra (1,3) and the Euclidean algebra (3,0), fall within this range, ensuring that our results are broadly applicable while avoiding the intractability of higher-dimensional cases. For example, in robotics and electromagnetism, the use of (3,0) or (1,3) algebras suffices for most modeling needs, while larger signatures are rarely encountered in practice.\n\nOur approach generalizes previous work on quaternions [3] and Clifford algebras [4] by providing a unified treatment for all grades in GA, rather than focusing on specific subalgebras or assuming commutativity. For instance, the quaternionic Laplace transform is limited to four-dimensional algebras and typically does not address bivector or trivector components, while Clifford algebra approaches often require the function to be monogenic or restrict attention to scalar-valued transforms. In contrast, our definition applies to arbitrary multivector-valued functions and respects the full multigrade structure, with the Laplace transform applied linearly and grade-wise. By explicitly restricting the Laplace parameter to scalars or complex numbers, we avoid ambiguities arising from non-commutativity, which are not addressed in some earlier works.\n\nA key challenge in transforming multivector-valued functions is the non-commutative nature of the geometric product. Our definition ensures that the Laplace transform is applied linearly and grade-wise, so that the relationships between grades are preserved. The scalar exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) commutes with all multivector components, as justified above, so the transform is well-defined for all admissible functions. If the Laplace parameter \\( s \\) were a general multivector, the exponential would not, in general, commute with multivector components, leading to ambiguities; thus, our restriction on \\( s \\) is essential for consistency.\n\nAs with the classical Laplace transform, not all functions admit a Laplace transform in GA. The class of admissible functions consists of those whose grade components are of exponential order and piecewise continuous on every finite interval. For example, functions that grow faster than exponentially, such as \\( f(t) = e^{t^2} \\mathbf{e}_1 \\) (where \\( \\mathbf{e}_1 \\) is a basis vector), do not have a convergent Laplace integral. Similarly, if any grade component of a multivector-valued function has an essential singularity or a discontinuity on a set of positive measure, the Laplace transform may not exist for that component, and thus for the function as a whole. For further discussion of admissibility in the multivector context, see [2, Sec. 3.2].\n\nTo illustrate the practical benefits of our approach, consider the analysis of a three-phase RLC circuit where voltages and currents are represented as multivectors to encode both magnitude and orientation. In classical analysis, scalar and vector components are treated separately, and geometric relationships such as phase orthogonality must be enforced manually. Using our GA Laplace transform, the circuit equations\u2014comprising coupled differential equations for each phase\u2014can be transformed into the Laplace domain in a unified manner. This preserves the geometric relationships between quantities, such as orthogonality of phase currents, and allows for simultaneous analysis of scalar (e.g., total power) and vector (e.g., current direction) components. The result is a more compact and geometrically meaningful formulation compared to classical approaches, which often require separate handling of each component.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews relevant background and related work; Section 3 introduces the formal definition and main properties of the GA Laplace transform; Section 4 discusses applications and limitations, including the RLC circuit example; and Section 5 concludes with directions for future research.\n\nReferences:\n[1] Doran, C., & Lasenby, A. (2003). Geometric Algebra for Physicists. Cambridge University Press.\n[2] Hitzer, E. (2013). Multivector Differential Calculus. Advances in Applied Clifford Algebras, 23(1), 181\u2013195.\n[3] Ell, T. A., & Sangwine, S. J. (2007). Quaternion Fourier and Laplace Transforms for Signal and Image Processing. Advances in Imaging and Electron Physics, 140, 1\u201356.\n[4] Brackx, F., Delanghe, R., & Sommen, F. (1982). Clifford Analysis. Research Notes in Mathematics, 76."}}